# Release-surface review.
#
# This file used to guard an "independent release-qualification trust boundary"
# (#367 LPMCP-PRD-001 / B4) across four paths — the standalone verifier, the
# qualification runner, the promotion gate and the production-readiness
# contracts. All four were removed on 2026-09-13, and a CODEOWNERS rule on a
# path that does not exist protects nothing while reading as though it does.
#
# What remains is worth owning on its own terms: the release workflow publishes
# the artifact, this file decides who reviews, and Package.swift decides what
# gets built into it.
/.github/workflows/release.yml  @jordy33
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @jordy33
/Package.swift                  @jordy33

# The canonical index and the machinery that writes it.
#
# `docs/canon/index/*.tsv` is the table every quoted value is compared against, and
# `docs/canon/absence/*.u32` is what every absence claim is proved against. Both are generated by
# `Scripts/logic_canon.py build` on a machine with Logic installed, and both are plain tracked
# files that look exactly the same when they are edited by hand. `MANIFEST.json` digests them, and
# it is a tracked file too -- so an offline check cannot tell a build from a forgery committed
# alongside a matching manifest. Review 2026-09-15 demonstrated exactly that in three edits.
#
# These entries are, measured 2026-09-15, INERT: the branch ruleset has
# `require_code_owner_review: false` and `required_approving_review_count: 0`, which is the right
# setting for a solo maintainer who cannot approve their own pull request. They are kept as a
# statement of who owns these paths, and `docs/canon/README.md` says plainly that they are not a
# control today.
/docs/canon/                                 @jordy33
/Scripts/logic_canon.py                      @jordy33
/Scripts/check-canon-citations.py            @jordy33
